      "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nOn CPUs, DeepSeek-V4 raises `KeyError: 'c1a'` at the first decode step, since #56228. Every release from v0.30.0 to v0.33.0 is affected. The CPU `build_tile_scheduler` override omits the `c1a` and `c2a` keys that the shared sparse-SWA metadata builder reads. This PR returns them with the sentinel `None`, as the base builder does, and adds a regression test. Fixes #65364.\n\n## Claims\n\n- DeepSeek-V4 on CPU decodes again: a decode step through `DeepseekV4CPUSparseSWAMetadataBuilder.build()` no longer raises `KeyError: 'c1a'`.\n- The new test fails on main with exactly that error and passes with this change.\n- The CPU kernel CI step now also triggers on `vllm/models/deepseek_v4/cpu/` and `vllm/v1/attention/backends/mla/sparse_swa.py`, so a future change on either side runs the test.\n\n## Validation\n\n**Unit test** (`tests/kernels/test_deepseek_v4_cpu_kernels.py`), on main `66360a5cd0`:\n\n```\n# main (this PR's vllm/ changes reverted)\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/kernels/test_deepseek_v4_cpu_kernels.py::test_cpu_swa_builder_decode_step_reads_every_layer_type -v\nE       KeyError: 'c1a'\nvllm/v1/attention/backends/mla/sparse_swa.py:790: KeyError\nFAILED ...::test_cpu_swa_builder_decode_step_reads_every_layer_type\n\n# this PR\nPASSED ...::test_cpu_swa_builder_decode_step_reads_every_layer_type\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/kernels/test_deepseek_v4_cpu_kernels.py -q\n59 passed, 2 warnings in 42.57s\n```\n\n**End to end** on 2× AMD EPYC 9454 (2 NUMA nodes). DeepSeek-V4-Flash config with dummy weights: `load_format=\"dummy\"`, `hf_overrides={\"num_hidden_layers\": 4, \"num_nextn_predict_layers\": 0, \"quantization_config\": None}`, `dtype=bfloat16`, `--enforce-eager`.\n\n| Run | main `66360a5cd0` | this PR |\n|---|---|---|\n| TP1, `CpuPlatform` | `KeyError: 'c1a'` at the first decode step | decodes (TP1, checked with the same setup on `6d01893483`) |\n| TP1, `ZenCpuPlatform` | `KeyError: 'c1a'` at the first decode step (`sparse_swa.py:790`) | decodes |\n| TP2, `ZenCpuPlatform` | – | decodes |\n\nWith dummy weights the generated text is meaningless; the check is that decoding runs.\n\n**pre-commit** (full run) on the 3 changed files passes, including `ruff`, `mypy`, `typos`, the Buildkite step checks and `check-test-tethering`.\n\n## Details\n\n**Root cause.**\n- #55355 added the CPU backend, whose `build_tile_scheduler` returns `None` for `swaonly`, `c4a` and `c128a`.\n- A few hours later, #56228 (DeepSeek-V4.1) made `DeepseekSparseSWAMetadataBuilder.build()` also read `tile_sched[c1a]` and `tile_sched[c2a]` (`sparse_swa.py:790-791`), and extended the base `build_tile_scheduler` to all five types. The CPU override was not updated.\n- GPUs use the base method, so only CPU fails.\n\n**Why CI missed it.** The CPU kernel step runs `test_deepseek_v4_cpu_kernels.py`, but no existing test drives a decode step through the metadata builder. The step also did not trigger on the files involved. This PR fixes both.\n\n**Alternative.** The override could return `super().build_tile_scheduler(0)`, which yields the base's all-`None` sentinel and would track future layer types automatically. I kept explicit keys to match the existing code; happy to switch if preferred.\n\n**Not a duplicate.** No open issue or PR reports this (searched: DeepSeek-V4 CPU, `KeyError 'c1a'`, `build_tile_scheduler`).\n\n**AI assistance was used** for the investigation, the change and test, and the validation runs. I reviewed every changed line and the results.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n",
